1
00:00:00,029 --> 00:00:03,779
Numbering helps people connect an item
with its operational process.

2
00:00:04,150 --> 00:00:08,310
In our Chicago Fire demonstration
department, calls, records and

3
00:00:08,310 --> 00:00:10,140
work orders have separate settings.

4
00:00:10,569 --> 00:00:15,699
From Department Settings, open Calls &
Dispatch, then Call Settings for calls.

5
00:00:16,059 --> 00:00:18,430
Document Numbering is under Customization.

6
00:00:18,889 --> 00:00:23,360
For records, open Records, expand Manage
and choose Settings.

7
00:00:23,790 --> 00:00:28,520
Work orders, invoices, bids and daily time
reports share Document Numbering.

8
00:00:28,950 --> 00:00:33,259
Check the page before editing: saving an
invoice pattern does not change

9
00:00:33,259 --> 00:00:35,169
call or incident-record numbering.

10
00:00:35,399 --> 00:00:38,219
Open Call Settings and scroll to Call
Numbering.

11
00:00:38,729 --> 00:00:48,719
Our saved pattern is FD51-{YYYY}-{SEQ}: a
department prefix, full year and sequence.

12
00:00:49,089 --> 00:00:50,630
Sequence digits is four.

13
00:00:51,059 --> 00:01:01,450
The inline example uses sample sequence
153, so it reads FD51-2026-0153; that

14
00:01:01,450 --> 00:01:03,700
sample is not the next issued number.

15
00:01:04,079 --> 00:01:07,030
The sequence token must appear exactly
once.

16
00:01:07,459 --> 00:01:11,369
Letters, digits and supported separators
form the fixed text.

17
00:01:11,830 --> 00:01:16,200
Review the example, choose Update and
reopen the page to check the stored

18
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:18,330
format before creating a training call.

19
00:01:18,539 --> 00:01:21,099
Read the token guidance under Call number
pattern.

20
00:01:21,610 --> 00:01:24,269
A year pattern restarts each numbering
year.

21
00:01:24,670 --> 00:01:28,969
Adding month makes a monthly scope; adding
day makes a daily scope.

22
00:01:29,389 --> 00:01:32,229
A month needs a year, and a day needs a
month.

23
00:01:32,570 --> 00:01:36,379
Without a date, the sequence continues
without a date-based restart.

24
00:01:36,799 --> 00:01:44,389
For example, AC-{YYYY}{MM}{DD}-{SEQ} is a
daily format.

25
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:48,170
We keep the saved yearly FD51 pattern
here.

26
00:01:48,549 --> 00:01:51,700
Dates follow the department time zone, so
check that setting

27
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:53,310
before choosing a reset rule.

28
00:01:53,519 --> 00:01:55,439
Review Numbering year starts.

29
00:01:55,869 --> 00:02:00,030
Our saved month is January and the day is
one, so this example

30
00:02:00,030 --> 00:02:01,440
follows the calendar year.

31
00:02:01,919 --> 00:02:05,879
A yearly format starts a new sequence at
its numbering-year boundary.

32
00:02:06,309 --> 00:02:10,530
Before using a different boundary, review
the intended year label and the page's

33
00:02:10,530 --> 00:02:12,710
help against your department's convention.

34
00:02:13,220 --> 00:02:16,190
Monthly and daily call patterns use
calendar dates.

35
00:02:16,560 --> 00:02:19,370
We are leaving the fiscal settings
unchanged and checking

36
00:02:19,370 --> 00:02:20,839
the displayed next number.

37
00:02:20,989 --> 00:02:24,430
Next call number is the next value for a
call logged now.

38
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:30,730
Saving our new FD51 format started its own
scope at 0001; older

39
00:02:30,730 --> 00:02:32,449
call numbers remained intact.

40
00:02:32,890 --> 00:02:37,459
After the training call received 0001,
this page shows 0002.

41
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:42,800
Raise next sequence to is for carrying
forward numbers issued before Resgrid.

42
00:02:43,209 --> 00:02:44,699
It can only move forward.

43
00:02:45,119 --> 00:02:50,259
Save pattern or year-boundary changes
first, reopen, then review any separate

44
00:02:50,259 --> 00:02:52,529
carry-forward value against the new scope.

45
00:02:52,969 --> 00:02:57,169
Leave this field blank when the displayed
next number is correct, as we do here.

46
00:02:57,319 --> 00:03:01,810
In an authorized training exercise, open
the saved call and compare its issued

47
00:03:01,810 --> 00:03:03,529
number with the format you reviewed.

48
00:03:04,010 --> 00:03:10,139
Our disposable training call received
FD51-2026-0001.

49
00:03:11,269 --> 00:03:15,370
An older call keeps its original 26-format
number because a pattern

50
00:03:15,370 --> 00:03:17,550
change never rewrites issued numbers.

51
00:03:17,960 --> 00:03:20,839
Mixed historical formats can therefore be
expected.

52
00:03:21,359 --> 00:03:24,939
If a result differs from what you
expected, check the local date,

53
00:03:25,259 --> 00:03:27,070
numbering-year boundary and scope.

54
00:03:27,529 --> 00:03:32,009
Do not create repeated calls merely to
force a counter toward the inline sample.

55
00:03:32,199 --> 00:03:35,110
In Records Settings, find Record
numbering.

56
00:03:35,529 --> 00:03:40,000
We preserve {PREFIX}-{YYYY}-{SEQ}

57
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,279
with four digits and January first.

58
00:03:42,710 --> 00:03:44,989
PREFIX uses the record type prefix.

59
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:49,059
Training defaults to TRN when its override
box is blank.

60
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:56,760
The next-number table shows TRN-2026-0002
after the existing training record.

61
00:03:57,239 --> 00:04:02,459
Prefixes shared by types share a sequence;
GROUP can create group-specific scopes

62
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:04,389
and must be followed by a separator.

63
00:04:04,770 --> 00:04:07,520
Calls month and day tokens do not belong
here.

64
00:04:07,869 --> 00:04:11,150
Review stored settings and each relevant
next row before

65
00:04:11,150 --> 00:04:12,669
changing this separate family.

66
00:04:12,850 --> 00:04:14,609
Open the existing training record.

67
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:23,890
Its issued number is TRN-2026-0001, its
station is Firehouse 51

68
00:04:24,180 --> 00:04:25,930
and its state is Finalized.

69
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:30,170
The register also has a separate Work
draft with a draft reference.

70
00:04:30,580 --> 00:04:33,009
These are different types and lifecycle
states.

71
00:04:33,420 --> 00:04:38,000
A number identifies an item; it does not
by itself establish review,

72
00:04:38,179 --> 00:04:40,099
approval or external submission.

73
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:42,619
Check the state and history separately.

74
00:04:43,119 --> 00:04:46,930
Use an existing safe example when one is
available, preserving its

75
00:04:46,930 --> 00:04:48,880
original contents and audit trail.

76
00:04:49,079 --> 00:04:50,510
Open Document Numbering.

77
00:04:50,959 --> 00:04:55,809
Each row controls work orders, invoices,
bids or daily time reports.

78
00:04:56,170 --> 00:05:02,100
For our training work request we saved
MNT-{YY}-{SEQ} with

79
00:05:02,100 --> 00:05:03,949
four digits, using Update.

80
00:05:04,319 --> 00:05:10,000
The sample reads MNT-26-0153, while the

81
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,710
saved next value initially read 0001.

82
00:05:13,259 --> 00:05:19,140
After the request was issued, it advanced
to MNT-26-0002.

83
00:05:19,830 --> 00:05:23,789
Invoice, bid and time-report rows retain
their built-in formats.

84
00:05:24,179 --> 00:05:28,359
This page has its own numbering-year
boundary, separate from Calls and Records.

85
00:05:28,729 --> 00:05:32,630
Reopen after saving and inspect every row
you intended to change.

86
00:05:32,749 --> 00:05:36,810
The work-order wizard asks what the
request is for, its unit or group,

87
00:05:37,129 --> 00:05:40,219
costs, safety details and a final review.

88
00:05:40,719 --> 00:05:44,650
Our fictional numbering demonstration uses
Other, Low priority,

89
00:05:44,919 --> 00:05:46,969
no assignment and no costs.

90
00:05:47,449 --> 00:05:53,359
Saving creates MNT-26-0001 in Requested
state.

91
00:05:53,779 --> 00:05:56,000
Reopen its detail and compare the

92
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:57,549
register: older WO

93
00:05:57,549 --> 00:05:59,189
numbers remain unchanged.

94
00:05:59,549 --> 00:06:04,049
This request is software practice, not a
completed repair or safety clearance.

95
00:06:04,490 --> 00:06:09,530
Records Settings separately holds request,
occupancy, inspection, permit,

96
00:06:09,740 --> 00:06:11,999
investigation and evidence numbers.

97
00:06:12,479 --> 00:06:15,699
Verify actual saved items for the families
you change and

98
00:06:15,699 --> 00:06:17,530
retain the chosen reset rules.

99
00:06:17,969 --> 00:06:22,519
A community care or mental health team can
use a clear follow-up record prefix while

100
00:06:22,519 --> 00:06:24,260
keeping service work orders separate.

101
00:06:24,740 --> 00:06:28,679
Search and rescue teams can distinguish
mission records from equipment requests.

102
00:06:29,139 --> 00:06:32,679
Security teams can separate patrol
records, work requests

103
00:06:32,740 --> 00:06:33,949
and client documents.

104
00:06:34,420 --> 00:06:38,120
Wildland contractors can check fiscal-year
rules for time reports.

105
00:06:38,569 --> 00:06:42,380
Industrial teams can distinguish incident,
inspection, permit

106
00:06:42,469 --> 00:06:43,840
and maintenance references.

107
00:06:44,309 --> 00:06:48,499
In each setting, the identifier describes
the item; review its actual

108
00:06:48,499 --> 00:06:50,849
contents and lifecycle state separately.

